[
  {
    "id": "c-1785661207278-wt4i",
    "pageId": "III-3",
    "chapterId": "III",
    "lang": "en",
    "blockIndex": 3,
    "blockId": "III.3.2",
    "quote": "Remark 3.2",
    "comment": "Add a translator note (both Fr and En): Let (Y\\subset X) be closed, let (U=X\\setminus Y), and let (F) be a sheaf on (X). For every open set (V\\subset X), there is a restriction map\n\n[\nH^i(V,F)\\longrightarrow H^i(V\\cap U,F).\n]\n\n## 1. Statement of Remark 3.2\n\nLemma 3.1 says that the vanishing\n\n[\n\\underline H_Y^q(F)=0\\qquad(q<n)\n]\n\nis equivalent to the following two conditions:\n\n* the restriction map is an isomorphism for (i<n-1);\n* it is injective for (i=n-1).\n\nRemark 3.2 says that, when (n\\ge 2), the second condition is unnecessary.\n\nIn other words, assume that for every open (V\\subset X),\n\n[\nH^i(V,F)\\longrightarrow H^i(V\\cap U,F)\n]\n\nis an isomorphism for all (i<n-1). Then the map in degree (n-1),\n\n[\nH^{n-1}(V,F)\\longrightarrow H^{n-1}(V\\cap U,F),\n]\n\nis automatically injective.\n\nThus, for (n\\ge2), it is enough to check the isomorphisms in degrees strictly below (n-1).\n\n## 2. The issue with the original proof\n\nThe original proof uses the fact that the presheaf\n\n[\nV\\longmapsto H^r(V,F)\n]\n\nhas zero associated sheaf when (r>0). This is because every positive-degree cohomology class becomes zero after restricting to a sufficiently small open neighbourhood.\n\nHowever, the proof applies this observation to\n\n[\nV\\longmapsto H^{n-2}(V,F).\n]\n\nThis causes a problem when (n=2). In that case (n-2=0), so the presheaf is\n\n[\nV\\longmapsto H^0(V,F)=F(V).\n]\n\nIts associated sheaf is (F), which is generally not zero.\n\nTherefore, the original argument works only when (n-2>0), namely when (n\\ge3). It does not justify the important borderline case (n=2).\n\nThe statement of the remark is still correct; only the proof needs to be modified.\n\n## 3. A corrected proof\n\nFor every open (V\\subset X), the long exact sequence for cohomology with support in (Y) contains\n\n[\nH^{n-2}(V,F)\n\\longrightarrow\nH^{n-2}(V\\cap U,F)\n\\longrightarrow\nH^{n-1}_{Y\\cap V}(V,F)\n\\longrightarrow\nH^{n-1}(V,F).\n]\n\nBy assumption,\n\n[\nH^{n-2}(V,F)\\longrightarrow H^{n-2}(V\\cap U,F)\n]\n\nis an isomorphism, and in particular it is surjective. Exactness therefore implies that\n\n[\nH^{n-1}_{Y\\cap V}(V,F)\n\\longrightarrow H^{n-1}(V,F)\n]\n\nis injective.\n\nThese injections are compatible with restriction, so there is an injection of presheaves\n\n[\n\\left(V\\mapsto H^{n-1}_{Y\\cap V}(V,F)\\right)\n\\hookrightarrow\n\\left(V\\mapsto H^{n-1}(V,F)\\right).\n]\n\nNow (n\\ge2), so (n-1>0). Hence the presheaf\n\n[\nV\\mapsto H^{n-1}(V,F)\n]\n\nhas zero associated sheaf. After sheafifying the injection above, we obtain\n\n[\n\\underline H_Y^{n-1}(F)\\hookrightarrow 0.\n]\n\nThus\n\n[\n\\underline H_Y^{n-1}(F)=0.\n]\n\nThe same long exact sequence, using the assumed isomorphisms in lower degrees, also gives\n\n[\n\\underline H_Y^q(F)=0\n\\qquad(q<n-1).\n]\n\nTherefore\n\n[\n\\underline H_Y^q(F)=0\n\\qquad(q<n).\n]\n\nLemma 3.1 now implies that\n\n[\nH^{n-1}(V,F)\\longrightarrow H^{n-1}(V\\cap U,F)\n]\n\nis injective for every open (V).\n\nThe correction is simple: instead of claiming that the presheaf (H^{n-2}(-,F)) has zero sheafification, one embeds the local-cohomology presheaf into (H^{n-1}(-,F)). Since (n-1>0), this latter presheaf does have zero sheafification, including when (n=2).\n You may shorten this as you see fit",
    "createdAt": "2026-08-02T09:00:07.278Z"
  },
